## Part 2

> Start part 2 please

Strategy step 2: generate mock CRM data with Python, computing KPIs, LTV
clusters, engagement and year boundaries into a single static JSON file.

Shell commands run:

    cd analytics && uv init --name analytics --no-workspace --bare
    uv run python generate.py        # writes output/crm.json and ../app/public/data/crm.json

> So, please review this Data part as its the most important for the project. See
> if theres any implications with actual customer behaviour for car rentals. QA
> everything it was done and provide feedback. do not implement anything

Delivered a written review (realism + QA). No code changes.

> Please implement it to part 2 as recommended

Reworked generate.py per the four review recommendations:
  1. 2023 is now a near-full baseline year   -> 2024 YoY gross is +~17%, not +112%
  2. Frequent repeaters can span calendar years (concentrated / sustained / seasonal
     profiles) -> ~7% of customers now rent across >1 year; cohort 12+ column has data
  3. LTV decoupled from rental count via a per-customer lognormal price level
     -> LTV tiers form a gradient (High/Med/Low repeat 72% / 16% / 2%)
  4. Seasonal share raised toward a leisure-realistic mix
Trade-off accepted and documented in meta.assumptions: interior years 2024-2025
read ~39% (bounded-window effect); 2026, the default view, reads ~31%.

    uv run python generate.py        # re-run after the rewrite

> That make sense [keep seasonal share as is] ... shouldnt the data for Oct 25
> be already [visible]? ... yes, make that change then start Phase 3

Fixed the cohort-matrix cliff: concentrated repeater bursts may now start any month
and cross one year boundary, so every first-rental month carries a realistic repeat
mix (was ~all one-timers for Oct-Dec cohorts). Added a short early-2027 continuation
tail (never shown in the UI) so December 2026 is a real month, not a truncated edge.
Re-tuned: 2026 reads 33% / B2P 23%, interior years low 40s, ~13% of customers rent
across more than one calendar year, LTV tiers a gradient (High/Med/Low 65/22/3%).

    uv run python generate.py        # re-run after the cohort fix

